+# Decision: Preview cache eviction policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker keeps a private cache of rendered contact sheets, bounded to 12 GiB of JPEG bytes. FrameDesk's editors (24 on one worker during the pilot) generate two opposing workload patterns against that cache: long sequential screenings introduce large volumes of one-use sheets in chronological order, while comparison sessions repeatedly revisit a small working set of clips across a week. A typical sheet is 2 MiB, so the budget holds roughly 6,000 sheets — comfortably larger than a comparison working set, small enough that a single screening can sweep the whole cache.
+
+The eviction policy decides which sheets leave when the budget is exceeded. Getting it wrong means comparison sessions re-render the same clips repeatedly (450 ms render each, plus queue delay) or, worse, renders thrash while a screening walks the archive. The policy is private to the preview worker: whatever is chosen, the `GET /internal/previews/{clip-id}/{revision}` contract, the FIFO render queue's concurrency limit, and the disk budget are unchanged.
+
+This decision assumes the caching model in `docs/intent/previews/previews-design.md`: entries keyed by immutable clip revision and renderer version, admitted after render completion, never authoritative copies of source footage.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Gate (inherited): stays inside the worker.** No change to the HTTP interface, job-ticket semantics, render-queue concurrency, or any cross-node behavior. All three options pass; it bounds the blast radius of choosing and later re-choosing.
+- **Gate (inherited): bounded bookkeeping.** Replacement metadata must stay proportional to cache size and private to the worker; no unbounded counters or per-request global statistics. All three options pass.
+- **Scan resilience — major.** A long sequential screening must not evict the sheets a comparison session is actively revisiting. This is the criterion the workload mix turns on: whichever policy handles it defines whether editors wait on re-renders.
+- **Reuse retention — major.** Sheets requested repeatedly (a comparison working set) should stay cached as long as they stay hot.
+- **Adaptation to project switches — moderate.** When editors switch productions, the cache should transfer to the new working set without carrying stale favorites forward. Derives from the tenet that a cache miss is ordinary behavior, not an API failure: a cache that lags behind current work turns every miss into a repeat miss.
+- **Bookkeeping cost on the hit path — moderate.** The worker serves 24 editors on eight cores; per-hit work beyond a pointer move or two competes with rendering.
+- **Tuning surface — minor.** Fewer and less-sensitive parameters are cheaper to get right before pilot telemetry exists.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU
+
+**Description.** A single queue of cache entries ordered by recency of last request. Every hit moves the entry to the front; eviction removes the least-recently-used tail until the byte budget admits the new sheet. No auxiliary structures.
+
+**Demonstration.** During a screening, each newly requested sheet becomes the most-recently-used entry; the comparison sheets a team revisited an hour earlier drift one position toward eviction per screening request and are gone after the cache turns over once.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Stays inside the worker: **passes**.
+- Bounded bookkeeping: **passes** — one doubly-linked list.
+- Scan resilience: **weak** — a sequential scan is the worst case for recency ordering; it rewrites the entire ordering and evicts everything older than the scan.
+- Reuse retention: **partial** — a reused sheet is retained only as long as no scan intervenes; exactly the case the workload produces.
+- Adaptation: **strong** — recency tracks current work immediately.
+- Hit-path cost: **strong** — a pointer move per hit.
+- Tuning surface: **strong** — no parameters.
+
+**Summary.** Cheapest to run, and it fails precisely on the scan-versus-reuse interaction that dominates this workload.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+**Description.** Each entry carries an access counter. Hits increment the counter; periodically (or on every hit), all counters decay by a fraction of their value, so counts reflect recent frequency rather than lifetime totals. Eviction removes the lowest-count entry.
+
+**Demonstration.** A clip requested forty times over the past week keeps the highest counter and survives a screening sweep; a clip requested three times yesterday decays toward parity with never-requested entries as the screening continues.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Stays inside the worker: **passes**.
+- Bounded bookkeeping: **passes** — one counter per entry plus decay work.
+- Scan resilience: **strong** — one-off scan entries never accumulate frequency, so they evict first.
+- Reuse retention: **strong** — repeatedly popular clips hold the highest counts.
+- Adaptation: **weak** — aging trades freshness for stability; a project switch leaves yesterday's high counts dominant for a decay horizon, retaining favorites past their useful life.
+- Hit-path cost: **partial** — counter update plus aging maintenance on every hit or tick.
+- Tuning surface: **weak** — the decay rate interacts with request rates and must be tuned before any telemetry exists.
+
+**Summary.** Best raw retention of popular clips, paid for in counter maintenance and a lag in following an editor population that switches projects.
+
+### 2Q
+
+**Description.** The 2Q policy partitions entries by demonstrated reuse. A1-in is a FIFO queue for sheets admitted on first appearance, bounded to a fraction of the cache budget. Am is an LRU queue for sheets requested at least twice, holding the rest of the budget. A1-out is a byteless FIFO of keys recently evicted from A1-in without promotion ("ghost list"): a re-request of a ghosted key proves reuse, and the sheet is admitted directly to Am. Hits promote A1-in entries to Am and move Am entries to its front. Eviction drains A1-in's FIFO tail first, then Am's LRU tail. (This design's instantiation: A1-in at 3 GiB — 25% of budget — ghost list at 2048 keys.)
+
+**Demonstration.** A screening walk fills A1-in and ghosts its keys as they fall off; the comparison sheets promoted to Am are untouched, because eviction reaches Am only after A1-in is exhausted. A clip requested once, evicted, then requested again during the same screening is recognized via its ghost key and re-enters as an Am member.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Stays inside the worker: **passes**.
+- Bounded bookkeeping: **passes** — two queues plus a fixed-capacity key list.
+- Scan resilience: **strong** — one-off sheets die in A1-in and never touch Am.
+- Reuse retention: **strong** — twice-requested sheets leave scan-contested space entirely.
+- Adaptation: **partial** — A1-in's FIFO turnover means a project switch's new sheets occupy Am only after a second request; no stale favorites are retained, but nothing is pre-warmed either.
+- Hit-path cost: **partial** — promotion moves and ghost-list membership checks on admission; still pointer-level work.
+- Tuning surface: **weak** — the A1-in/Am byte split and ghost-list depth are parameters that shift behavior and need tuning against real workloads.
+
+**Summary.** Buys scan-proof reuse protection with two queues and a size split that must be tuned.
+
+## Selection
+
+2Q is selected. The two major criteria both reward separating first-use from reused entries, and 2Q is the only option that makes that separation structural rather than a matter of counters (frequency/aging) or luck (LRU). LRU's failure mode — a single screening sweeping the comparison working set — is the exact interaction the workload guarantees will occur; frequency/aging avoids it but pays a persistent maintenance cost to end up lagging project switches, a moderate criterion, while its tuning surface is as large as 2Q's. Against the hit-path criterion, 2Q's extra bookkeeping is promotion and ghost checks — bounded, pointer-level, off the render path — and the accepted cost of the protection.
+
+Committing to 2Q obligates: the A1-in byte ceiling and ghost-list depth ship as constants (3 GiB, 2048 keys) and are tuned against pilot telemetry before being treated as settled; the tuning question is open in the owning LLD. It forecloses nothing outside the worker — the HTTP contract, queue, and budget are untouched, which is also what keeps this decision cheap to revisit if the workload mix shifts. The decision turns on the workload's scan-versus-reuse tension, and honors the tenets that cache paths and replacement bookkeeping stay private to the preview worker and that a miss is ordinary behavior: under 2Q a scanned-over one-off re-rendering on its second request is an ordinary miss, not a policy failure.
